1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
Open Planner to set up one connected group activity.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:06,000
Lilian uploads her InfoVis slides.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,500
She chooses a voting story whose shrinking maps filter groups of people.

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:14,500
She asks for an editorial review: inspect area, labels and sequence, then write a shared critique.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:17,500
She selects Role Play for the group activity.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,500
Three teams will work on the same activity.

7
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,500
Four learners per team will receive four distinct role interfaces.

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,500
The activity has eighteen minutes.

9
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,500
The Planner summarizes the setup before generation.

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,500
The canvas contains one instructor plan and four student interfaces. Open any interface to inspect it.

11
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:42,500
Request group discussion, separate role work, then shared writing. Preview the connected change and apply it.

12
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,500
Choose contribution, peer response and reasoning checks. Individual answers remain available as activity data.

13
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,500
Before the shared critique, the Reader’s question should receive a linked response.

14
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,500
When initial choices match, record a response to a contrasting claim and a peer check.

15
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:01,500
Run scripted browser tests for saving, reload, private answers, peer access and handoffs. All twenty-five checks pass.

16
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:07,500
One class QR leads to group and role selection.

17
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:12,500
Each student chooses a group, claims an available role and opens the assigned interface.

18
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:16,500
Simulated records show three collaboration situations. Open a team to inspect its evidence.

19
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:23,500
Three of four contributions are saved with two minutes left. Eli has no saved contribution and explicitly asks for smaller steps.

20
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:27,500
Compare Eli’s current and proposed interfaces before applying any change.

21
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,500
Inspect the original role tools and task requirements.

22
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:36,500
The same role task now has three smaller steps. Eli completes them; teammates can use the shared findings.

23
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:38,500
Only Eli receives the smaller-step task. Existing work stays intact.

24
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:43,500
The teacher applies the change and opens Eli’s actual updated interface from the canvas.

25
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:49,500
Example follow-up work shows the smaller steps and a saved role contribution. The teacher reads the work and records a review.

26
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:55,500
Team 2 reaches shared writing. Sam’s question about population size and political power has no linked response.

27
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:00,500
The proposed interface shows Sam’s question beside the critique and provides a direct response field.

28
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,500
Apply only to Team 2. Preserve the original question and critique.

29
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:09,500
An example response from Alex addresses Sam’s question and cites the legend. The teacher still checks its quality.

30
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,500
All four initial choices match, but the selected contrast-and-reply check is missing. Agreement itself is not a wrong answer.

31
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:20,500
Add a counterfactual: could the map shrink while total residents stay constant? Ask for an explanation and a peer response.

32
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:23,500
Team 3 receives the reasoning exchange. Original individual answers stay private.

33
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:28,500
Example work includes Ren’s explanation and Taylor’s linked reply. The teacher inspects both before recording a review.

34
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:31,500
Detect a concern, preview a change, apply it to the relevant interfaces, and inspect what happens next.

35
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:35,500
The knowledge-state report separates demonstrated performance, areas needing support, and evidence still needing review.

36
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:40,500
The teacher reviews Eli’s individual explanation and records what it shows, plus a new task to check independently.

37
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:45,500
Maya’s saved answer still treats map area as land area. Her team finishing the activity does not erase this individual difficulty.

38
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,500
Save a class snapshot with task evidence and teacher judgments. The report can also be downloaded for later review.
